---

<Doc
  title="Wallyt privacy policy"
  eyebrow="Wallyt"
  eyebrowHref="/wallyt"
  updated="2026-10-04"
  product={product('wallyt')}
  description="What Wallyt collects, who can see it, who we share it with, and how to delete it."
>
  <p>
    Wallyt is an app for splitting shared costs. It is made by {company.brand} ({company.legalName}; “we”, “us”). This policy covers the Wallyt app and Wallyt on the web. It explains what we collect, why, who can see it, and how you can delete it.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>In short:</strong> we collect what Wallyt needs to work: your name, email, the groups
      you’re in and the expenses you record. The people in your groups can see what you share with
      them. We don’t show ads, we don’t use analytics or tracking tools, and we never sell your data.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What we collect</h2>

  <h3>Your account</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Email address</strong>, used to sign you in and to match you to groups you’ve been added to.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Name</strong>, as you want it shown to the people in your groups. Each group keeps its own
      copy, so you can be known by a different name in each.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Photo</strong>, if you choose to add one, for your account or for one group. We crop and
      resize it on your device before it is uploaded.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sign-in details.</strong> If you sign in with Google, we receive your name, email address,
      Google account ID and profile photo from Google, and a new account starts with that photo. If
      you sign in with Apple, we receive your name and email address from Apple. We never receive or
      store a password.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Your groups and expenses</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Group names and group photos.</li>
    <li>
      Expenses and payments: descriptions, notes, categories, amounts, currencies and the exchange
      rates used, dates, who paid and how each one is split.
    </li>
    <li>A history of changes to each expense: who changed it, when, and what changed.</li>
    <li>
      For repeating expenses, your time zone offset, so each copy is added on the right day where
      you live.
    </li>
    <li>
      If you import a spreadsheet, such as a Splitwise export, the expenses in it, plus a record of
      the import: the file name, how many rows it had and how its columns were matched. The file is
      read on your device and only those are sent to us. Exporting a group makes the file on your
      device and sends nothing to us.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>People you add</h3>
  <p>
    When you add someone to a group, we store the name you give them and, if you add them from
    your contacts, their email address. If you are the group’s owner or an admin, the contact’s photo
    is saved as their photo in that group. When that person later joins Wallyt with the same email
    address, they take over their place in the group and can change their own name and photo.
  </p>

  <h3>Contacts on your phone</h3>
  <p>
    Wallyt asks for access to your contacts only when you choose to add people from them, and it
    only ever reads them. The list is shown on your phone. Nothing is uploaded except the people
    you pick, as described above. Phone numbers are never sent to us: when you text someone an
    invite, the number goes straight to your phone’s messaging app.
  </p>

  <h3>Photos on your phone</h3>
  <p>
    Wallyt can open your photo library so you can choose a picture for yourself, someone in a
    group, or a group. Only the photo you choose is uploaded. On newer Android phones the library
    includes photos backed up to Google Photos, and a photo you pick from there is downloaded to
    your phone first. Wallyt does not use your camera.
  </p>

  <h3>Technical information</h3>
  <p>
    Like any online service, our servers receive your IP address and basic details of each request,
    such as the time and the page or action asked for. We use them only to keep Wallyt running and
    secure, for example to limit repeated attempts to guess invite links, and our server logs are
    deleted after a few days.
  </p>

  <h3>What we don’t collect</h3>
  <p>
    We don’t collect your location, your bank or card details, your device’s advertising ID, or
    information about other apps. Wallyt doesn’t move money: it records what people owe each other,
    and you settle up however you like.
  </p>

  <h2>How we use it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>To run Wallyt: show your groups, work out balances, add repeating expenses and keep the history.</li>
    <li>To sign you in, including emailing you sign-in codes.</li>
    <li>To convert between currencies at the right day’s exchange rate.</li>
    <li>To keep Wallyt secure, for example by limiting repeated attempts to guess invite links.</li>
    <li>To answer you when you contact us.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We don’t use your data for advertising, we don’t build profiles of you, and we don’t use it to
    train AI models.
  </p>

  <h2>Who can see your information</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>People in your groups</strong> can see your name, email address and photo, and every
      expense, payment, note and change in the groups you share.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Anyone with a group’s invite link</strong> can see a preview of that group before joining:
      its name, photo and currency, and the names and photos of people in it. Email addresses are never
      shown in the preview. Only share an invite link with people you want in the group.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Photos</strong> are stored at addresses that can’t be guessed, but anyone who has the
      address of a photo can open it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Us</strong>, when we need to: to keep Wallyt running, to fix a problem you’ve asked us
      about, or when the law requires it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who we share it with</h2>
  <p>We never sell or rent your personal information. We use a small number of service providers:</p>
      <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Provider</th><th>Why</th><th>What they receive</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Our hosting provider</td>
          <td data-label="Why">Runs the servers Wallyt’s data is stored on</td>
          <td data-label="Receives">Everything described in this policy, stored on our behalf</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Our email delivery provider</td>
          <td data-label="Why">Sends your sign-in codes</td>
          <td data-label="Receives">Your email address and the code</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Google</td>
          <td data-label="Why">Sign in with Google, if you use it</td>
          <td data-label="Receives">
            A sign-in token, which we check with Google. On the web, Google’s sign-in prompt loads on
            the sign-in screen, so Google receives your browser’s standard request details there even
            if you don’t choose Google
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Apple</td>
          <td data-label="Why">Sign in with Apple, if you use it</td>
          <td data-label="Receives">Your sign-in request</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Frankfurter and Currency API</td>
          <td data-label="Why">Daily exchange rates</td>
          <td data-label="Receives">Only currency codes and dates, never anything about you</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  <p>
    We may also disclose information if the law requires it, or to protect the safety of our users
    or the public. If {company.legalName} is ever sold or merged, Wallyt’s data would move with it,
    and this policy would keep applying until you’re told otherwise.
  </p>

  <h2>What’s stored on your device</h2>
  <p>
    Wallyt keeps your sign-in session in your phone’s secure storage, and a few preferences in the
    app’s own storage: the last email you typed, the group you last opened, your appearance setting,
    a pending invite, and recent exchange rates. On the web, these are kept in your browser’s local
    storage. Wallyt doesn’t use advertising or tracking cookies.
  </p>

  <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
  <p>
    We keep your account for as long as you have it. Groups, expenses and their history are kept
    until the group is deleted. When someone leaves or is removed from a group, their name, email
    address and photo stay with that group’s past expenses, so everyone else’s balances remain
    correct.
  </p>
  <p>
    When you delete your account, we delete it within 30 days. See <a href={url('/wallyt/delete-account')}>Delete your Wallyt account</a> for exactly what is removed and
    what stays with your groups.
  </p>

  <h2>Security</h2>
  <p>
    Everything between Wallyt and our servers is encrypted in transit. Wallyt has no passwords to
    leak: you sign in with a one-time code that expires after 10 minutes, or with Google or Apple.
    Groups are visible only to their members. Only a group’s owner and admins can remove people,
    and only its owner can delete it. No system is perfectly secure, but we work to protect your data and will
    tell you if a breach affects you.
  </p>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>
    Wallyt is not meant for children under 13, and we don’t knowingly collect information from them.
    If you believe a child under 13 has given us information, email <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a> and we will delete it.
  </p>

  <h2>Your choices and rights</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      You can change your name and photo in Wallyt at any time, from Account, or for one group from
      your own row in its settings.
    </li>
    <li>You can export any group’s expenses as a CSV file.</li>
    <li>You can leave a group at any time.</li>
    <li>
      You can ask us for a copy of your data, ask us to correct it, or ask us to delete it, by
      emailing <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a> from the email address you
      sign in with.
    </li>
    <li>You can delete your account. See <a href={url('/wallyt/delete-account')}>Delete your Wallyt account</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Depending on where you live, privacy laws such as the California Consumer Privacy Act or the GDPR
    may give you further rights, including the right to object to how we use your data and to
    complain to a regulator. We honor these requests wherever you live, and we will never treat you
    differently for making one. We reply within 30 days.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>
    If we change this policy, we’ll update the date at the top. If a change affects how we use your
    information in a meaningful way, we’ll let you know by email or in Wallyt before it takes effect.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    {company.brand} ({company.legalName})<br />
    <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a>
  </p>
</Doc>
